<section class="section section--tight" aria-labelledby="export-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="export-title">Exporting without paying tax first</h2>
      <p>Rule 96A of the CGST Rules lets a registered person export without paying integrated tax, if a bond or Letter of Undertaking in Form GST RFD-11 is furnished before export. The undertaking has deadlines attached.</p>
      <ul class="tick-list plain-list">
        <li><strong>Goods:</strong> tax and interest fall due if goods are not exported within 15 days after three months from the invoice date, or any further time allowed</li>
        <li><strong>Services:</strong> the same if payment in convertible foreign exchange, or in rupees where permitted, is not received within 15 days after one year from the invoice date, or any further time allowed</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="para">So keep a list of export invoices with their shipping details and payment dates. A filing is only as good as that list. Check the rule's current wording, since this copy is dated.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Rejected orders</h2>
      <p>Business Standard reported in 2021 that Okhla garment units had sold rejected goods at around 10% of their price after orders stopped during the pandemic. A rejection affects more than sales:</p>
      <ul class="tick-list plain-list">
        <li>Goods return to stock at a much lower value</li>
        <li>Export paperwork for the shipment may need correcting</li>
        <li>Credit taken on the inputs may raise questions</li>
        <li>Money owed by the buyer may never come</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="para">Record the reason, the buyer's message and the resale price at the time. It is hard to rebuild afterwards.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

